# [Applications and serving APIs]($heading.id('serving-apis'))

Applications and agents send requests to LLMD over HTTP. The endpoint determines
what the server returns.

| Endpoint | Result |
| --- | --- |
| [Chat completions]($link.page('llmd/api/chat-completions')) · `/v1/chat/completions` | An OpenAI-compatible client or SDK sends messages. LLMD returns a completion or streams the generated text. |
| [Embeddings]($link.page('llmd/api/embeddings')) · `/v1/embeddings` | The model pools its output on the device and returns a vector for each input text. |
| [Decision]($link.page('llmd/api/decision')) · `/v1/systemone` | The client supplies one state and a set of yes/no, choice or score questions. A question can depend on an earlier answer. |

For observability, LLMD exposes Prometheus metrics and provides crash reports.
See [Monitor and troubleshoot LLMD]($link.page('operations/monitoring').ref('scrape-service-metrics')) to inspect service metrics during a run.

# [Serving engine]($heading.id('engine'))

For chat, the engine renders a Jinja2 template in Zig and tokenizes the prompt.
The diagram lists BPE, WordPiece and SentencePiece. On the return path, output
parsers extract reasoning traces and tool calls from model output.

- **Scheduling and cache.** The scheduler continuously batches requests, mixes prefill and decode work, and can split long prefills into chunks. A paged KV cache holds attention state and enables prefix reuse.
- **Speculative decoding.** A drafter proposes tokens for the target model to check. The diagram names DSpark, DFlash and DSpine. Find [external drafters for your model]($link.page('llmd/models').unsafeRef('external-drafters')).
- **Execution and parallelism.** The host prepares the next step while the device runs. Tensor, expert and data parallelism distribute different parts of the work across devices.
- **Weight preparation.** The engine can fuse, stack or swizzle weights into the layout a kernel expects. The diagram gives FP8, MXFP4 and NVFP4 as examples.

See [Batching and memory]($link.page('llmd/cli/batching-and-memory')) for the flags that control scheduling and cache capacity.

# [DeepSeek V4.1-Flash example]($heading.id('deepseek-v4-1-flash'))

In this Zig model, token embeddings pass through repeated decoder layers.
The language-model head produces scores for sampling. The diagram also shows
the DSpark drafter for speculative decoding.

| Inside a decoder layer | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Engram | On selected layers, the host computes n-gram hashes for lookups in host-pinned tables. A gate controls how much of the result is added to the residual stream. |
| Sparse MLA attention | Latent query and KV projections use YaRN rotary embeddings. A KV compressor and top-k indexer support sliding-window and sparse attention with attention sinks, followed by a grouped output projection. |
| Mixture of experts | A router selects the top-k MXFP4 routed experts. The layer also has a shared expert and can distribute experts across devices. |
| mHC | Mixes residual streams before and after attention and expert computation. |
| Paged caches | Window KV uses FP8. Compressed KV and indexer keys use FP4. |

The [model compatibility catalog]($link.page('llmd/models')) lists the published launch recipes for this model.

# [Kernel implementations]($heading.id('kernels'))

An operation can use a custom kernel for the platform or a portable StableHLO
implementation. The table gives the five operation families in the diagram.
Attention includes MLA and GQA. The indexer examples are DSA and QSA.
A dash means the diagram leaves that implementation unspecified.

| Platform | Attention | Indexer | Gated DeltaNet | Mixture of experts | mHC |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| NVIDIA | CuTe · Triton | Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe |
| AMD | Fly · Triton | Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly |
| Google TPU | Mosaic | StableHLO | Mosaic | Mosaic | StableHLO |
| Trainium | NKI | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Intel | Triton | StableHLO | Triton | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Apple | Metal | StableHLO | Metal | Metal | StableHLO |
| Furiosa | TCL | StableHLO | TCL | TCL | StableHLO |
| CPU | StableHLO | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |

# [ZML compilation and execution]($heading.id('zml'))

ZML compiles the model code into an executable and loads the weights into
device buffers for that executable.

- **Tensor API and compiler.** Zig model code expresses operations such as matrix multiplication, gather, scan and sort. For example, `q.dot(k, .hd).softmax(.k)` computes a dot product followed by softmax. ZML generates MLIR using dialects including `stablehlo`, `sdy` and `func`, with one program per shape bucket. See the [Tensor API]($link.page('zml/api/tensor')).
- **Kernel languages.** The compiler can embed custom kernels in the graph. The diagram names Triton, CuTe, CUDA Tile, FlyDSL, Mosaic, NKI, Metal and TCL.
- **Sharding.** Tensor shardings express how data is placed on a device mesh with named axes. XLA partitions the computation accordingly. See [Run ZML across devices]($link.page('zml/sharding')).
- **I/O.** ZML reads tensors from Hugging Face, S3, Google Cloud Storage, HTTP or disk and moves data between host and device using DMA. See [Load and inspect model weights]($link.page('zml/checkpoints')).
- **Execution and PJRT.** The runtime launches the compiled executable asynchronously with device buffers. The PJRT C API gives ZML a common plugin interface for compilation, data transfer and execution. See [Exe]($link.page('zml/api/exe')) and the [OpenXLA source](https://github.com/openxla/xla).

# [Platforms]($heading.id('platforms'))

The diagram pairs framework targets with runtimes and kernel languages. To
deploy a server, check the [LLMD distributions]($link.page('llmd/reference/compatibility'))
and the [model recipes]($link.page('llmd/models')) for your hardware.

| Framework target | Runtime or architecture | Kernel languages shown |
| --- | --- | --- |
| NVIDIA | CUDA | CuTe, CUDA Tile, Triton |
| AMD | ROCm | FlyDSL, Triton |
| Google TPU | libtpu | Mosaic |
| AWS Trainium | Neuron | NKI |
| Intel | oneAPI | Triton |
| Apple | Metal | Metal |
| Furiosa | Furiosa SDK | TCL |
| CPU | x86, Arm | — |
